==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|resuscitātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|resuscitō||to raise up again, revive}}, from {{m|la|re-||again}} + {{m|la|suscitō||to raise up}}, from {{m|la|sub-||up, under}} + {{m|la|citō||to summon, rouse}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈsʌsɪˌteɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-resuscitate.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{hyph|en|re|sus|ci|tate}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[restore]] [[consciousness]], [[vigor]]{{,}} or [[life]] to.
#: {{ux|en|to '''resuscitate''' a drowned person}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''resuscitate''' withered plants}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Moya Lothian-McLean|title=It’s Not Going Well for Britain’s New Prime Minister|work=The New York Times|date=2023-01-30|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/01/30/opinion/britain-rishi-sunak.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Rishi Sunak, Britain’s prime minister, has a plan for the new year. In a speech in early January, he set out an agenda to '''resuscitate''' the country and save the Conservative Party, now in free fall.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[regain]] [[consciousness]].
#: {{syn|en|come to}}

